 "title": "1 in a million, 1 step: HashMap",
 "sources": [
  "Java SE 21 HashMap API docs (https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/21/docs/api/java.base/java/util/HashMap.html, fetched 2026-11-24): 'constant-time performance for the basic operations (get and put), assuming the hash function disperses the elements properly among the buckets'; default load factor .75; when entries exceed load factor times capacity, 'the hash table is rehashed... so that the hash table has approximately twice the number of buckets'; 'using many keys with the same hashCode() is a sure way to slow down performance of any hash table'.",
  "Arithmetic: binary search over 1,000,000 sorted items needs about 20 comparisons (2^20 = 1,048,576)."
 ],
 "lines": [
  {
   "text": "Find one key among a million in a Java HashMap. Does it check a million items, twenty, or one? Guess.",
   "caps": "*1 IN A MILLION | 1M, 20, OR *1? | *GUESS"
  },
  {
   "text": "Usually one. The key's hash code tells it exactly which bucket to look in.",
   "caps": "USUALLY *ONE | *HASH CODE | WHICH *BUCKET"
  },
  {
   "text": "So get and put run in constant time, no matter how big the map grows.",
   "caps": "*GET AND PUT | *CONSTANT TIME"
  },
  {
   "text": "Once it's seventy five percent full, it rebuilds with about twice the buckets.",
   "caps": "*75% FULL | *REBUILDS | *2X BUCKETS"
  },
  {
   "text": "That keeps every bucket short, so lookups stay fast.",
   "caps": "SHORT *BUCKETS | STAYS *FAST"
  },
  {
   "text": "But here's the catch: that only works if the hashes spread out.",
   "caps": "THE *CATCH | HASHES MUST *SPREAD"
  },
  {
   "text": "Give many keys the same hash code, and they pile into one bucket, and every lookup slows down.",
   "caps": "SAME *HASH CODE | ONE *BUCKET | *SLOW"
  },
  {
   "text": "So write a good hash code for your keys. Did you guess one?",
   "caps": "GOOD *HASHCODE | DID YOU GUESS *ONE?"
  }
 ],
